    label: 'CIVIC AXIS / COLLECTIONS / ALPINE WEATHER',
    tagline: 'Choose a civic base before moving to an alpine gateway.',
    hubIntro: 'Canberra’s civic districts and the Snowy Mountains are separate operating trips, not one city excursion. Begin by choosing which side of Lake Burley Griffin anchors each day: Parliament House and the national institutions in Parkes; the National Museum and West Basin on Acton Peninsula; or the Australian War Memorial and the Mount Ainslie foothills in the north. They look close on a map, but the lake, broad roads, open ceremonial lawns and bus corridors make each a distinct half-day or full-day block. Put a timed tour, ticketed exhibition or Last Post Ceremony first, then arrange the nearby walk around that fixed point. A bridge or path closure can change which shoreline connection is practical, so do not plan a crossing from a photograph or a straight line alone.\n\nUse the city as a base for the institutions and choose a separate day for Namadgi only after checking park alerts, fire conditions, road access and a suitable vehicle or confirmed operator. The Snowy Mountains need a deliberate move to a New South Wales gateway such as Jindabyne, Thredbo or Perisher; the correct base depends on the season and exact trail, lift or resort. A regional coach to Jindabyne does not solve onward transport to every park entrance. Keep the high country as its own leg with a spare weather day, current chain and road rules, and a return plan that still works if a lift or seasonal shuttle is not running. In this guide, “Australian Alps” means a set of separate access systems, not one connected city day trip.',
    searchSummary: 'Plan Canberra’s civic institutions as separate city days, then move to Jindabyne or another alpine gateway for the Snowy Mountains. Check city buses, bridge detours, booked coaches, park access and weather before fixing the sequence.',
    searchKeywords: ['Canberra city itinerary', 'Canberra bus routes 53 54 57 58', 'Canberra to Jindabyne coach 775', 'Snowy Mountains seasonal shuttle'],
    stay: 'Allow four Canberra nights for three full days across Parliament House and one Parkes institution, the National Museum and West Basin, and the War Memorial with its optional summit. Three nights usually gives two full days plus arrival/departure windows, so choose two districts and keep the third for a shorter stop only if actual timing permits. A central, Parkes or Acton base can reduce some transfers, but it does not make the lake edge, parliamentary precinct and northern memorial a single compact walking district. Compare the nearest useful stop and the day’s final return before choosing a hotel only for its map position. For an ACT nature day, allow the return drive and park access check; do not count on a city bus to reach every trailhead. For southern Kosciuszko, sleep in Jindabyne; choose Thredbo or Perisher only when that specific resort, lift or trail is the goal. Keep the alpine gateway for multiple nights because a coach arrival, local connection and changeable mountain weather can consume a day. A Canberra hotel is not a practical daily base for alpine roads.',
    transfer: 'Treat city routes and regional travel as separate systems. The Transport Canberra timetable in effect on 12 October 2026 lists Route 53 for the National Museum corridor and Routes 57/58 to Parliament House; its visitor routes guide lists R54 for the War Memorial. Verify the date, direction, stop name, weekend service and return departure in the live planner. The civic axis is broad and exposed, so an accessible vehicle transfer between selected institutions may be a better choice than a long walk in heat, wind or rain. NSW TrainLink’s Southern Region timetable valid from 26 July 2026 lists the advance-booked Route 775 coach from Canberra Civic to Jindabyne. Check the exact boarding point, booking availability, luggage terms and arrival time before building onward travel. From Jindabyne, confirm a separate vehicle, operator or seasonal service for the chosen park area; do not assume the Canberra coach reaches a resort trailhead. The published 2026 Snowy Mountains Bus Service ran 6 June–4 October, so that ski-season service had ended by this 12 October review. The northbound side of Commonwealth Avenue Bridge is closed to road and shared-path users from 8 January 2026, with reopening anticipated by the end of January 2027, weather permitting; follow the current National Capital Authority detour. A timetable snapshot is useful for planning, but recheck it for the actual travel date.',
